    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d793">

                <pb facs="0688" n="679" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg679"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">859A.01/46: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-05-11T17:00:00+02:00">May 11, 1940—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1302. My 1176, May 1, 1 p.m.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d793-f1"/> Yesterday’s and this
                    morning’s press published reports of the British occupation of Iceland which is
                    pictured as an unprovoked aggression against a small and utterly defenseless
                    people and thus as demonstrating the hypocrisy of England’s righteous
                    indignation against Germany for its action to protect the neutrality of other
                    small nations from British encroachment. The DAZ<anchor n="5" xml:id="d793-f2"/>
                    this afternoon publishes a brief editorial which argues that Iceland is closer
                    to Greenland in which the United States has manifested interest than to Scotland
                    and therefore is to be regarded as in the Western Hemisphere and as falling
                    within the scope of the Monroe <pb facs="0689" n="680" xml:id="pg680"/>
                    Doctrine. The British action which is described as a side show to divert public
                    attention from British failures on the Continent is said accordingly to be of
                    primary interest to the United States and not to Germany.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
